 (Round to two decimal places.)

The element of the sample space is 210, because if you have more than two 1s it must be that the two 1s are to the left of the decimal, giving the biggest number you can make, 110. And the smallest number you can make is 101. If there is 1 1 and 1 0, you can only add to the 1, you can't subtract from it, so you get the answer, 110. It makes sense because any number between 110 and 120 would include elements of the sample space, so the number 120 is not the largest possible number, because there is another number that would have more.

You cannot assume that the final number ends with an 0, because for example, if it ended with an 1, you would think that you have the answer, 110, but the first case cannot have an 1.

You cannot assume it to be a terminating decimal because the answer has a floor of 110, and the largest number possible from a sample space is 120.

When you do a test of significance for multiple proportions you want to keep the sequences with the lowest p-value. As a result we rank all the p-values, and there are no ties since all numbers are unique. We select the the ones with the lowest p-values. Then we do a post hoc test where we test each of the sequences against the other sequences for each group to decide if the difference between these groups are significant.

You will want to check if your sample space is balanced or unbalanced, meaning that the outcomes are equally likely. For the latter, an outcome "100" will count as two outcomes, for example. To check your sample space, you will need to use a hash, which records the number of occurrences. Here is a little function that checks your sample space:

def check_balance(s):
    counts = {x: [0] * len(s) for x in s}
    for i in range(len(s)):
        for j in range(len(s)):
            if s[i][j] == 1:
                counts[i][j] += 1
    if len(counts) != len(s):
        return False
    else:
        for i in range(len(s)):
            if counts[i][i] != 1:
                return False
    return True

