#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  int n;
  double co2_emissions, volume_per_tin, liters;

  printf("Enter the number of liters in one can of Dr. Pepper: ");
  scanf("%lf", &volume_per_tin);

  printf("Enter the total amount of CO2 emissions during 2022 (in metric tons): ");
  scanf("%lf", &co2_emissions);

  co2_emissions *= 1000; // convert from metric tons to kg
  n = (int)(co2_emissions / volume_per_tin);

  if (co2_emissions > n * volume_per_tin) { // check if there is any leftover emissions
    liters = (co2_emissions - (n * volume_per_tin));
  } else {
    liters = 0;
  }

  printf("The volume of room-temperature Dr. Pepper that could be carbonated is approximately %.2f liters.\n", liters);

  return 0;
}
```

In this program, we first take two inputs from the user, the number of liters in one can of Dr. Pepper and the total amount of CO2 emissions during 2022 (in metric tons). We then convert the CO2 emissions from metric tons to kilograms and calculate the number of cans of Dr. Pepper that can be carbonated. If there are any leftover emissions, we add them to the total volume of carbonated Dr. Pepper.

Note that the number of cans of Dr. Pepper that can be carbonated may not be a whole number, so we need to use an integer variable `n` to store the result. We then check if there are any leftover emissions and adjust the volume accordingly. Finally, we output the result with two decimal places using `printf`.

