 We're given the value '2.564' in the problem.

We can start by writing out a list of nearest powers of 10 and their exponents:
\begin{aligned} 10^{-1} &= 0.1\\10^0 &= 1\\10^1 &= 10\\10^2 &= 100\\10^3 &= 1000\\10^4 &= 10^4 = 10^6 \end{aligned}

Now we need to figure out the exponent for the nearest power of 10 that is greater than or equal to 2.564 but has a lower exponent than 2.564. 2.564 is 1 tenth of a power of 10. We need a power of 10 less than 1 tenth of a power of 10.

So, we need to subtract 1 tenth from 2.564. A tenth of a power of 10 has an exponent of 0. So we need to subtract 1 from an integer with an exponent 0. We can write this as:
\begin{aligned} 2.564 - 0.1 &= 2.464\\ \end{aligned}
2.464 is 2 tenths of a power of 10. We want the nearest number in the list of powers of ten with an exponent less than 2. Let's call that nearest number 'x'.
\begin{aligned} 2.464 &= x\\ \end{aligned}
We'll now use the following definition of near: if 'a' and 'b' are any two numbers, we say 'a' is near 'b' when the absolute value of the difference of 'a' and 'b' is less than 0.001. So, if we check this definition, we can see that 2.464 is near 2.564 (the difference is less than 0.001).
\begin{aligned} 2.464 - 2.564 &= -0.100\\ \text{Since} | -0.100 | < 0.001, \text{we can say} \ \ 2.464 \text{is near} \ 2.

