 It's $\dfrac{51}{500}$. Assuming each positive integer, $n$, has an equal chance to be chosen, we have

\begin{align*}\frac{\binom{5}{51}}{\binom{5}{50}} &= \frac{\binom{5}{51}}{\binom{5}{49}}\\& = \binom{5}{51} + \binom{5}{50} = \frac{1}{500}\end{align*}

since $\frac{\binom{5}{51}}{\binom{5}{49}}= 0$.The reason why we added the factorials was to ensure that we had taken all cases of all elements of each index into account. (The total number of ways to choose $5$ elements out of $5$ is $\frac{5!}{5-5!} = \frac{1}{5!}$.

