1) My biggest objection to your code is that it is rather random. It is not at all clear what the intent is.

2) Your code will not work for 128-bit numbers (or bigger). Since my proposal will give a "round up" number for exactly the number of bits that there are in your number (0, 1 or 2^N bits), for N above 2^63 you need to either do some trickery in your comparison method, or use a different method (one that checks 128-bit numbers will have been invented when we get around to the next speculative idea!).

3) You omit the code for computing the 128-bit integer exponent using the method described in the spec.

My language is C. However, the only new bits are that the compiler defines a language runtime that includes a reasonable assumption of the base overflow or underflow algorithms. The language defines these operations.

The rest is just conversion between types, same as in C, except that the cast to integer is standard, not a new or exotic feature, and the implicit conversion to integer is undefined, but it works for negative values.

Any platform with a native 64-bit type, and any 64-bit integer values or variable length integer types, can run the code, with the round functions working as specified.

