Solution: The key word here is nearest. We want to reduce the minimum exponent to just a whole number, in the following example:

45/50 is a decimal that is really 0.9 rounded up to the nearest hundredth. But we want the exact answer, and you'll see below why. We want to know how far up we can round.

Using the ratio as our approximation, let's move one step at a time. Suppose we round to the nearest tenth first. Then we have

\begin{align}
0.90 \approx 1.00
\end{align}

and we can eliminate the second $0.9$ and get

\begin{align}
9 \approx 10.
\end{align}

That's why we rounded up from $0.9$. Then, we want to get rid of the extra $1$ we created, by going back to $0.9$. You may be able to work out from this, but I'll do the rest to make sure we understand what's happening. Going back to $0.9$ means we get

\begin{align}
90 = 100 - 10 - 1.
\end{align}

By doing this, we lost our unit fractions in the fractional parts, so we know we can round the decimal down from $0.9$ to $0.8$ without changing the amount. That means we've done:

\begin{align}
8 = 10 - 1.
\end{align}

Now if we want to round to the nearest thousandth, we only have a value of $0.8$ to deal with. This means:

\begin{align}
8 \approx 9.
\end{align}

That's because it's still a lot more than $8$ millionths. So we now have

\begin{align}
10 - 8 = 2.
\end{align}

That means we can now use the value $9$ for the whole thing. Finally, that means:

\begin{align}
10^2 = 10^1 + 2,
\end{align}

so the least power of ten we could ever need is $2$. We're done. The answer is $2$.

More examples:

\begin{align}
6 \approx 6.5,
\end{align}

