In the last post in this series, we finished converting their output into a standardized text. Now that you've seen their output, we can investigate the rules (in the bibliography provided at the end of this post) and start to reconstruct their base.

The resulting data is not JSON, but a small C++ library for rapid conversion of LaTeX to JSON called latex2json can make this pretty easy to manage. The code is available on GitHub and used here:

The precise rules for converting their table to Latex are:

• A space is a comma
• A tab is a tab
• use \ \ to put two periods on a line
• use & to enter a reference to another cell
• Use ${}^{22}$ to denote units: Watts.
• Denote units by placing the exponent after the number: 10^2. Put a space between "Watts" and the exponent.
• Use {{}} to put double parentheses around a string. Enclose units in {0.} to remove the space between.
• Adjust the precision of the numbers to two significant figures.
• Place a " " before a letter, and don't include space after the letter.
• Place a " <" before a number, and don't include space after the number.

The reason the numbers are not accurate and have spaces is because the Space Shuttle uses both Newton and Watts to describe their value. These units are abbreviated as N and W, so a space is needed to indicate between those two.

For example, 10000 might be written as 1.0000$\times$10,4 or 10^4. Look for the number itself, the exponential form, or a combination of the two.

