Solution:

The way to think of this is as a heat transfer problem.  In this case, a relatively small amount of energy ($$E$$) heats a large mass of ice ($$m$$).  The rate of this heat transfer is known because we know that the power of the supernova is $$P = 10^{34}$$ Watts.

So,

\begin{align*}
E = P\cdot t\\
E = 10^{34}\cdot t\\
\end{align*}

The heat transfer rate due to this is:

\begin{align*}
\dot{Q} &= \dot{m}\cdot c\cdot \Delta T\\
\dot{Q} &= \frac{E}{t}\cdot c\\
\dot{Q} &= \frac{10^{34}}{t}\cdot c\\
\end{align*}

Here we plug in $$c$$ for the heat capacity of ice:

\begin{align*}
\dot{Q} &= \frac{10^{34}}{t}\cdot c\cdot 2800 J/kg/ºC \\
\dot{Q} &= \frac{10^{34}\cdot 2800}{t}\ J/ºC
\end{align*}

We are interested in the temperature change:

\begin{align*}
\Delta T &= \frac{10^{34}\cdot 2800}{t}\cdot \frac{1}{1000}ºC\\
\Delta T &= 28 \cdot \frac{10^{23}}{t}\ºC\\
\end{align*}

The mass of Antarctica is approximately $$m = 2 \cdot 10^{14}\text{ kg}$$.  The final temperature can be expressed as:

\begin{align*}
T_f = 0 + 28\cdot \frac{10^{23}}{t} \\ T_f = 28 \cdot \frac{10^{23}}{t} \text{ºC} = 28 \cdot 10^{23} \text{ºC}\\
\end{align*}

So:

\begin{align*}
\Delta T &= 28 \cdot \frac{10^{23}}{t} \\

