 Since (1) $10^9$ is too large, (2) $10^8 = 10^{15} / 10^7$ is too large, and (3) $10^7 = 10^{14} / 10^7$ is too small, we need a ninth digit. To obtain the ninth digit, set $\text{total}/10^8$ = 1, which would require 1,719,543,316 cows, or about $\lfloor 2\cdot 10^8\rfloor = 2 \cdot 10^9$.

This is not correct, because we only need 8 digits of precision. The value of total should be close to 1,500,000,000, which yields

\begin{align} \text{total}/10^8 &= 1 \\ \frac{10^9}{\text{total}} &= 10^8 \\ \end{align}

This yields 8 significant digits in the ratio (which requires that we round up):

\begin{align} \frac{\lfloor 1.00000001\cdot 10^9\rfloor}{\lfloor 1.00000002\cdot 10^9\rfloor} &= 10^8 \\ \frac{1.00000001\cdot 10^9}{\lfloor 1.00000002\cdot 10^9\rfloor} &= 10^8 \\ 10^9\cdot 1.00000001 &\leq \lfloor 1.00000002\cdot 10^9\rfloor \\ 10^9 &\leq 100 \\ 1,000,000,000 &< 10^9 \\ \end{align}

