Question 2: Suppose the die rolls we make in question 1 are not independent, and instead depend on the trials before us. If two players start the game, there is a 1/2 chance they will both survive until the third trial, where they will both fail, resulting in a score of 0. What is the chance both players fail on the fourth trial, where we actually have 19 die rolls remaining? (c) 0.98

Answer: There are $\binom{37}{0} \binom{12345579}{19} \binom{119}{19} \binom{2}{19}$ possible pairs of players who would both be under 18 in the remaining 19 dice rolls. Out of those pairs, there are $\binom{37}{0} \binom{119}{19}$ ways to have one player survive at least once. Thus, the probability that both players fail in round 4 is \begin{aligned} \frac{ \binom{37}{0} \binom{12345579}{19} \binom{119}{19} \binom{2}{19} }{ \binom{37}{0} \binom{119}{19} } = \frac{1}{20921504}. \end{aligned}

