# Use Factorial

The closest answer is using the natural log of the factorial as described by @MetaJustin. However, you can look even further by using the fact that the Gamma function is the natural log of the factorial:

\begin{align} \Gamma(n + 1) & = (n!)^{1/n} \\ \ln(Gamma(n + 1)) & = \frac{1}{n} ln((n!)^{1/n}) \\ \ln(Gamma(n + 1)) & = \frac{1}{n} ln(n!) - 1/n \\ ln(Gamma(n + 1)) & = (n - 1)ln(10) + \frac{1}{n} ln(n!) \\ \end{align}

Using the usual expansion for the factorial as follows:

\begin{align} \ln(Gamma(n + 1)) & = (n - 1)ln(10) + \frac{1}{n} \left(1\cdot 2\cdot 3\cdot \dots \cdot n\right) \\ \ln(Gamma(n + 1)) & = (n - 1)ln(10) + \frac{1}{n} n \ln(n) \\ \ln(Gamma(n + 1)) & = (n - 1)ln(10) + \ln(n) \\ \end{align}

Therefore: $\frac{\ln(Gamma(n + 1))}{n-1}

