We could treat the same issue as a mountain climber attempting to scale a very steep mountain. One usually thinks of a graph like that of the height v.s. time. For a moment let us consider it as a function of time: when going 1m the person slows down to roughly \frac 1t. But if we consider the function height versus time, where it goes up \frac 1t, in time t, and then back down \frac 1t. But since the function never actually reaches 0 it will go up forever. (This is wrong, but you could imagine someone climbing a mountain indefinitely) Here is one good illustration: \includegraphics[height=2.25in]{gradient.png}

So for the base "1 meter" we could compute

$(1 + \frac {1}{2})^{1\cdot 10} (1+ \frac {1}{3})^{1\cdot 10} \cdots (1+ \frac {1}{11})^{1\cdot 10} \approx 2 \cdot 2.4 \cdots 2.718281829 \approx 5.299$

Here we took the sum of $2\cdot 2.4 \cdots 2.718281829$ and got \boxed{5.299}.

In a way this idea lends itself to thinking about arguments one would take to show that there are infinitely many primes.

