Question: How many Airpod earbuds would be required to cover the Moon?

Answer: 58

Question: How many Airpod earbuds would be required to cover the area of a basketball court?

Answer: 38

# Approach

1. First we need to calculate the surface area of the moon.
2. We’ll use the celsius formula: pi * radius ** 2 to calculate the surface area of the moon.
3. Then we subtract the surface area of one earbud to calculate number of earbuds for one circle that will cover whole surface of the moon.

# Solution

Code

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
double getMoonRadius(int version) {
// https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_moon_radii
return 1.728; //F-10 21.4cm
}
double getEarbudArea(double earbudRadius) {
return 3.14 * (earbudRadius) * (earbudRadius);
}
int main() {
int version = 0, power = 0;
cout << "Enter Bluetooth version: ";
cin >> version;
double moon = getMoonRadius(version);
cout << "Enter earbud size: ";
cin >> power;
cout << "Earbud area: " << getEarbudArea(power) << "cm^2" << endl;
cout << "Moon radius: " << moon << "cm" << endl;
double area = pow(10, power) * pow(10, power);
double n = ceil((moon - area)/area);
cout << "Earbuds required: " << n << endl;
}


# Time Complexity

We need to calculate the surface area of the moon. The surface area of the moon can be calculated with trigonometry, but because of the complex mathematical formula, it will take lots of time to execute.

Therefore we need to choose faster approach that will cover the complexity of such heavy mathematical calculation.

The time complexity of this code is : O(log n)

Hence the running time of our code will be less than 200 milliseconds which is absolutely great enough, provided that we have some good input.  Good luck with your Interview!!

